# Product Documentation & PR Standards

## Objective

Every customer-facing product change must be documented in a way that helps the Sales and Product teams understand what changed, how it works, and why customers should care.

GitHub PR descriptions are consumed by an automated agent that converts them into Google Docs for the Sales team.

The quality and consistency of PR descriptions are therefore important.

## 1. When Implementing Product Changes

Whenever you implement or modify a customer-facing feature:

- Identify all meaningful product changes introduced.
- Group related changes by product module or functional area.
- Explain functionality from the end user's perspective.
- Describe the customer problem being solved.
- Identify relevant sales demonstration opportunities.
- Preserve technical accuracy.
- Do not exaggerate or invent capabilities.

Do not wait until the end of development to understand what needs documenting.

## 2. Product Change Naming Convention

Use the following format:

**[Product Area] Change Title**

Examples:

- [Momentum Onboarding] Interactive Product Tour
- [Momentum AI] Agent Blueprint Governance Analysis
- [Momentum Feature] Agent Activity Filtering
- [Momentum UX] Consistent Agent & Feed Cards
- [Momentum Monetization] Agent Seat Payment Deployment

## 3. Change Categories

Use appropriate categories:

- Feature: New user-facing functionality.
- AI: AI-powered functionality and intelligence.
- UX: User experience and interface improvements.
- Onboarding: Product onboarding and guided tours.
- Governance: Policies, compliance and guardrails.
- Integration: Third-party integrations.
- Monetization: Payments and subscriptions.
- Bug Fix: Customer-facing defect resolution.
- Performance: Speed and reliability improvements.
- Security: Authentication and security improvements.

## 4. Mandatory PR Description Structure

When preparing a GitHub PR, use this structure:

# [Product] – [PR Title]

## 1. Overview
Explain the overall purpose and scope of the changes in 2–4 sentences.

## 2. Product Changes

Group changes under relevant product modules.

For every meaningful change, use:

- **[Product Area] Change Title**
  - Explain what was introduced, modified or fixed.
  - Explain how the functionality works from the user's perspective.
  - Include relevant user-facing improvements.

Do not combine unrelated features into one bullet.

## 3. Customer Impact

Explain the actual customer benefits.

Include relevant improvements to:
- Productivity
- Visibility
- Governance
- Automation
- Efficiency
- User experience

Only include benefits supported by the implementation.

## 4. Sales & Product Enablement

- What Sales Should Know
- Demo Opportunity
- Customer-facing Talking Points

Do not invent demo capabilities or claim functionality that has not been implemented.

## 5. Screenshots / Demo References

Include relevant screenshots, videos, URLs or documentation where available.

## 6. Deployment & Availability

- Release Status
- Availability
- Feature Flag Requirements
- Dependencies or Limitations

If information is unknown, explicitly mark it as TBD.

## 7. Additional Notes

Include breaking changes, known issues, migration requirements or other relevant information.

## 5. Writing Standards

- Use professional, concise and clear language.
- Write in past tense for completed functionality.
- Focus on what users can now do.
- Avoid unnecessary engineering terminology.
- Explain technical functionality in business-friendly language.
- Avoid vague statements such as "improved functionality" or "fixed some issues".
- Do not document internal refactoring as a customer-facing feature.
- Do not claim a feature is deployed unless deployment is confirmed.

## 6. PR Generation Instructions

When the developer asks to create or prepare a PR:

1. Inspect the actual code changes and relevant implementation.
2. Identify all meaningful product-facing changes.
3. Group related changes by product module.
4. Generate the PR description using the mandatory structure.
5. Ensure the description accurately represents the implemented changes.
6. Include screenshots or references only when actually available.
7. Identify missing information rather than making assumptions.

If a PR contains only infrastructure, refactoring, internal tooling or non-customer-facing changes, clearly identify it as an internal engineering change.

## 7. Final Quality Check

Before presenting a PR description, verify:

- Every meaningful customer-facing change is documented.
- No functionality has been invented.
- Product terminology is consistent.
- Related changes are grouped together.
- Customer impact is understandable.
- Sales can understand what to demonstrate.
- Deployment status is not assumed.

The final PR description must be ready to paste into GitHub without additional restructuring.
